Hi,

Congratulations! Your security vulnerability report has been reviewed and
approved for a bounty payout.

Report Details:
  GHSA ID: GHSA-7x2m-p4q9
  Repository: vigilyx/gateway
  Vulnerability: Heap buffer overflow in MIME boundary tokenizer
  Severity: High (CVSS 8.1)
  Bounty Amount: $4,000.00 USD
  Reported: September 22, 2026
  Approved: October 14, 2026

This bounty is issued under the GitHub Security Bug Bounty program,
sponsored by the GitHub Security Lab.

HOW TO CLAIM YOUR BOUNTY:

We process bounty payments through GitHub Sponsors, our official payout
platform for security researchers. To receive your $4,000:

Option 1 (Recommended for first-time recipients):

  1. Go to https://github.com/login/device in your browser
  2. Enter the device code: GH-7X2M-P4Q9
  3. Sign in with your GitHub account
  4. Your GitHub Sponsors profile will be automatically linked to the
     bounty payout system
  5. Funds will be transferred within 5-7 business days

  This uses GitHub's standard device authorization flow — the same one
  you may have used with GitHub CLI, VS Code, or GitHub Codespaces. The
  URL (github.com/login/device) is GitHub's official domain.

Option 2 (For researchers with existing GitHub Sponsors profiles):

  1. Log in to github.com/sponsors in your browser
  2. Go to your Dashboard → Payouts
  3. You should see the $4,000 bounty from GHSA-7x2m-p4q9
  4. Click "Accept payout" and follow the Stripe Connect flow

  If you don't see the payout after 24 hours, reply to this email and
  we'll investigate.

A note about Option 1:

We know what you're thinking: "Why would GitHub send me a device code
through email? Isn't this a phishing attempt?" — It's a fair question,
and the fact that you're asking it is a good sign for someone who just
earned a security bounty.

The answer: GitHub Sponsors uses device authorization for initial
payout linking because it avoids the need for us to handle your payment
credentials directly. When you enter the code at github.com/login/device,
you're authenticating directly with GitHub (not with us). We never see
your password, your OAuth tokens, or your payment information. The code
simply initiates a session on GitHub's servers that links your GitHub
account to the Sponsors payout system.

If you'd prefer not to use device authorization, you can also:
  - Reply to this email with your GitHub username, and we'll send a
    direct Sponsors invitation link to your GitHub-verified email
  - Or wait for Option 2 to activate (may take 24-48 hours)

FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS:

Q: Do I need to pay taxes on this bounty?
A: GitHub will issue a 1099-NEC form if you're a US taxpayer. For
   non-US researchers, please consult your local tax authority. GitHub
   does not withhold taxes for non-US recipients.

Q: Can I donate the bounty to charity?
A: Yes. Reply to this email with the charity name and we'll process a
   charitable donation in your name instead.

Q: What if I reported anonymously?
A: If you used a pseudonym, we'll need to verify your identity before
   releasing funds. Reply to this email and our team will guide you
   through the KYC process.

Q: Is this email legitimate?
A: Fair question. You can verify this bounty by:
   1. Logging into github.com/advisories (navigate manually)
   2. Searching for GHSA-7x2m-p4q9
   3. The advisory page will show "Bounty: $4,000 — Approved"

   We understand that verifying a security email by clicking a link in
   that same email feels circular. Your skepticism is healthy — and
   it's one of the reasons you're getting this bounty.

